<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · ideology</p><h1 class="enc-title">Liberalism</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1690 CE – present</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Liberalism is a political and moral philosophy of individual rights, liberty, consent of the governed and equality before the law, first systematised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-locke">John Locke</a> and carried by the revolutions of 1688, 1776 and 1789.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Liberal, liberty and libertine all derive from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> liber, meaning free. One of the first recorded uses of liberal, in 1375, described the liberal arts as an education desirable for a free-born man. The political tradition is mostly modern and began in the 17th century, although some of its ideas had precursors in classical antiquity and Imperial China. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-civil-war">English Civil War</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levellers">Levellers</a> called for freedom of religion, frequent parliaments and equality under the law.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-hobbes">Thomas Hobbes</a> built the idea of a social contract on a hypothetical state of nature and concluded that only an absolute sovereign could guarantee security. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-locke">John Locke</a>, generally regarded as the father of modern liberalism, took over the state of nature and the contract but argued that government needs the continuing consent of the governed. When a monarch becomes a tyrant, he held, the people have a right to overthrow him, since the contract protects life, liberty and property as natural rights.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Liberalism rests on individual rights, liberty, consent of the governed, political equality, private property and equality before the law. Different liberals understand these principles differently, and scholars often describe its doctrines as containing separate and often contradictory streams of thought. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adam-smith">Adam Smith</a>'s The Wealth of Nations (1776) held that where prices and competition are left free of regulation, the pursuit of self-interest, directed by an invisible hand, maximises a society's wealth. Jean-Baptiste Say and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-stuart-mill">John Stuart Mill</a> carried economic liberalism further, in 1803 and 1848.</p><p>Sismondi's New Principles of Political Economy (1819) was the first comprehensive liberal critique of early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">capitalism</a>. By the end of the 19th century poverty, unemployment and organised labour challenged classical liberalism, and social liberalism emerged. The word now varies by region. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a> it means a moderate form of classical liberalism, while in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> it refers almost exclusively to social liberalism.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glorious-revolution">Glorious Revolution</a> of 1688, which entrenched parliamentary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>, has been called the first modern liberal revolution. The American Revolution of 1776, the Constitution of 1787 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> of 1789 used liberal philosophy against royal sovereignty. In the 19th century liberal governments were established in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>, and liberal ideas influenced reform in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a>, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanzimat">Tanzimat</a> and Al-Nahda.</p><p>Liberalism has had many critics. The Roman Catholic Church was among its earliest and most outspoken. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edmund-burke">Edmund Burke</a> attacked the liberal faith in rationality and natural equality, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Karl Marx</a> rejected liberal theory. Before 1920 its main opponents were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conservatism">conservatism</a> and socialism, and it then faced fascism and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marxism-leninism">Marxism–Leninism</a>. Liberal ideas spread further in the 20th century, and social liberalism became a key component in expanding the welfare state.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Liberalism” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Czech historian František Palacký argued in 1848 that if Austria did not exist it would have to be invented, as a protection for the small nations of central Europe, and Austro-Slavism, Hungarian <b>liberalism</b> and Italian nationalism all took form in conflict with Vienna.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Article 13 of the Federal Act promised every state an assembly of estates, which led to constitutions in Bavaria and Baden in 1818 and Württemberg in 1819, but Metternich used the Diet to contain <b>liberalism</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-confederation">German Confederation · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Many officers were at first encouraged by the early liberal reforms of Tsar Alexander I, when <b>liberalism</b> was officially encouraged and expectations ran high.</blockquote><a href="/movement/decembrist-revolt">Decembrist revolt · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>Romantic writers turned against what they saw as the cold rule of reason, but the Enlightenment’s ideas lived on in <b>liberalism</b>, in the utilitarianism of Jeremy Bentham and in the sciences of society.</blockquote><a href="/movement/enlightenment">Enlightenment · Outcomes</a></li><li><blockquote>Lech Wałęsa and others brought together people associated with the Catholic Church and members of the anti-Soviet left, and Polish nationalism, together with pro-American <b>liberalism</b>, played an important part in its development.</blockquote><a href="/movement/solidarity">Solidarity · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>Ahmed Rıza wanted a centralized, Turkish-dominated empire free of European influence; Prince Sabahaddin a decentralized, federal state built on capitalism and <b>liberalism</b>.</blockquote><a href="/movement/young-turks">Young Turks · Aims</a></li></ul></section>
